eveloper/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071098/assets/2047d3b4bda0c588c7c26a6dd8e9b43b64b5e126cdc155282ee3d296a15554ec.png) So,\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071098/assets/16f0eedeee65a71dd9d8fa83535d5f65bff44f1c424663fb2b393046c131e696.png)\n\nSimilarly, to calculate ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071098/assets/9b1bb30112995dd5d0f3e6cd147c26736878279484f82d4d73a086b8efdaec9a.png) we take the number of green marbles that came from box ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071098/assets/27b99f04c6f0f2b24f2d107248a2315f6b2d2574f849bf47379d8f2fdd27d05e.png) ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071098/assets/b758dc5e36b9ce2daba82e94d58b5232cecff327cf85f3a2571675c5845ba90d.png) and divide by the total number of marbles in the mixture ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071098/assets/75c92641ef9ece42d3dab493a8e8fd78d276cd4f40e423f2b442d23464fb47c7.png) So,\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071098/assets/6ddd96143aa9754eb6b266d82b854a41fcd3e17d9d9c5aeec7d44a669e1994b3.png)\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected marble is green is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071098/assets/a1fb9fe4e3ce980bd3385ece84df7522afee0f98b5e57cf82e8c7e0a19a4119c.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071098/example-7863.json" "file_sha256" "fc5b2fa31faaee63e31321f11ec4fb984ac1dede3642a5eea0f529d01cb8e318"} {"question" "e-7862" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A certain disease is present in $\\dfrac{1}{5}$ of the population. A test to detect the disease gives a positive result in $\\dfrac{10}{11}$ of the people with the disease, and $\\dfrac{3}{11}$ of the people without the disease. What is the probability that a randomly selected patient tests positive for the disease?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071098/example-7862.json" "file_sha256" "73baf50527eb883024015af7e601ca46157d77c8652355465889bed9fc1b0e6e"} {"question" "e-7862" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $A$ be the event that a patient tests positive, and let $D$ be the event that the patient has the disease. Then $D'$ is the event that the patient does not have the disease.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected patient tests positive, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(A) = P(A|D)P(D) + P(A|D')P(D')\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since the disease is present in $\\dfrac{1}{5}$ of the population, we have\n\n$$\nP(D) = \\dfrac{1}{5}, \\quad P(D') = \\dfrac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\n- Since the test gives a positive result in $\\dfrac{10}{11}$ of the people with the disease, and $\\dfrac{3}{11}$ of the people without the disease, we have\n\n$$\nP(A|D) = \\dfrac{10}{11}, \\quad P(A|D') = \\dfrac{3}{11}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected patient tests positive is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A) & =P(A|D)P(D)+P(A|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{10}{11})(\\frac{1}{5})+(\\frac{3}{11})(\\frac{4}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{11}+\\frac{12}{55} \\\\  & =\\frac{10+12}{55} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071098/example-7862.json" "file_sha256" "73baf50527eb883024015af7e601ca46157d77c8652355465889bed9fc1b0e6e"} {"question" "e-7865" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A company manufactures electronic components, where $70\\%$ of the components are produced by machine $A,$ and the remaining $30\\%$ are produced by machine $B.$ Of the components produced by machine $A,$ $8\\%$ are defective, and of the components produced by both machines, $11\\%$ are defective. If a component produced by machine $B$ is randomly selected, what is the probability of being defective?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071098/example-7865.json" "file_sha256" "f549d2d82748725df882d363e5b6c2d8930202cb32cda17e04163c28edfc760d"} {"question" "e-7865" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $D$ be the event that the selected component is defective, and let $A$ be the event that the component was produced by machine $A.$ Then $A'$ is the event that the component was produced by machine $B.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected component is defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(D) = P(D|A)P(A) + P(D|A')P(A')\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since machine $A$ produces $70\\%$ of the components and $B$ the remaining $30\\%$, we have\n\n$$\nP(A) = 0.70, \\quad P(A') = 0.30.\n$$\n\n- Since $8\\%$ of the components produced by machine $A$ are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(D|A) = 0.08.\n$$\n\n- Since $11\\%$ of the components produced by both machines are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(D) = 0.11.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(D|A'),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′}) \\\\ 0.11 & =(0.08)(0.70)+P(D|{A}^{′})(0.30) \\\\ 0.110 & =0.056+0.3P(D|{A}^{′}) \\\\ 0.3P(D|{A}^{′}) & =0.054 \\\\ P(D|{A}^{′}) & =0.18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected component produced by machine $B$ is defective is $0.18.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071098/example-7865.json" "file_sha256" "f549d2d82748725df882d363e5b6c2d8930202cb32cda17e04163c28edfc760d"}] "usage" {"q-89054" [] "q-88980" [] "e-7863" [] "q-88578" [] "e-7865" [] "e-7862" []} "basis" 2791 "retractions" [[17592186083872 :question/problem "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nSophia has two email accounts, account $A$ and account $B$. She receives $35%$ of her emails in account $A$, and the remaining $65%$ go into account $B$. Approximately $15%$ of the emails received in account $A$ are spam while $25%$ of the emails received in account $B$ are spam. What is the probability that a randomly selected email is spam?"] [17592186083866 :question/problem "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nIn a class, $\\frac{1}{3}$ of the students are boys, and $\\frac{2}{3}$ are girls. Of the boys, $\\frac{1}{6}$ of them play soccer, and of the girls, $\\frac{1}{4}$ of them play soccer. What is the probability that a randomly selected student plays soccer?"] [17592186083873 :question/problem "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nA math teacher splits her calculus class into two groups, Group $A$ and Group $B$. Of the total students, $35%$ of her students are in Group $A$, and $65%$ are in Group $B$. If $20%$ of the students in Group $A$ obtained the top grade, and the probability that a randomly selected student from either group obtained the top grade is $0.265$, what is the probability that a randomly selected student from Group $B$ obtained the top grade? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places."] [17592186083032 :question/problem "In box $A$, there are $19$ marbles, $7$ of which are green. In box $B$, there are $21$ marbles, $8$ of which are green. The marbles from the two boxes are mixed, and one marble is randomly chosen from the mixture. What is the probability that the selected marble is green?"] [17592186083032 :question/worked-solution "Let $G$ be the event that the chosen marble is green, and let $A$ be the event that the chosen marble was in box $A$. Then $A'$ is the event that the chosen marble was in box $B$.\n\nTo calculate the probability that the randomly selected marble is green, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(G) = P(G\\cap A) + P(G\\cap A')\n$$\n\nTo calculate $P(G\\cap A)$, we take the number of green marbles in box $A$ $(7)$ and divide by the total number of marbles in both boxes\n\n$$\n(19+21=40)\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(G \\cap A) = \\dfrac{7}{40}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to calculate $P(G\\cap A')$, we take the number of green marbles in box $B$ $(8)$ and divide by the total number of marbles in both boxes $(40)$. So, we have\n\n$$\nP(G \\cap A') = \\dfrac{8}{40}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected marble is green is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(G) &= P(G∩A) + P(G∩A^{′}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{40} + \\frac{8}{40} \\\\\n&= \\frac{15}{40} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186083031 :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the event that a patient tests positive, and let $D$ be the event that the patient has the disease. Then $D'$ is the event that the patient does not have the disease.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected patient tests positive, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(A) = P(A \\mid D)P(D) + P(A \\mid D')P(D')\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since the disease is present in $\\dfrac{1}{5}$ of the population, we have\n$P(D) = \\dfrac{1}{5}, \\quad P(D') = \\dfrac{4}{5}$.\n- Since the test gives a positive result in $\\dfrac{10}{11}$ of the people with the disease, and $\\dfrac{3}{11}$ of the people without the disease, we have\n$P(A \\mid D) = \\dfrac{10}{11}, \\quad P(A \\mid D') = \\dfrac{3}{11}$.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected patient tests positive is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(A) &= P(A \\mid D)P(D) + P(A \\mid D^{′})P(D^{′}) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{10}{11})(\\frac{1}{5}) + (\\frac{3}{11})(\\frac{4}{5}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{11} + \\frac{12}{55} \\\\\n&= \\frac{10 + 12}{55} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186083033 :question/problem "A company manufactures electronic components, where $70\\%$ of the components are produced by machine $A$, and the remaining $30\\%$ are produced by machine $B$. Of the components produced by machine $A$, $8\\%$ are defective, and of the components produced by both machines, $11\\%$ are defective. If a component produced by machine $B$ is randomly selected, what is the probability of being defective?"] [17592186083033 :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the event that the selected component is defective, and let $A$ be the event that the component was produced by machine $A$. Then $A'$ is the event that the component was produced by machine $B$.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected component is defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(D) = P(D \\mid A)P(A) + P(D \\mid A')P(A')\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since machine $A$ produces $70\\%$ of the components and $B$ the remaining $30\\%$, we have\n$P(A) = 0.70, \\quad P(A') = 0.30$.\n- Since $8\\%$ of the components produced by machine $A$ are defective, we have\n$P(D \\mid A) = 0.08$.\n- Since $11\\%$ of the components produced by both machines are defective, we have\n$P(D) = 0.11$.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(D \\mid A')$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(D) &= P(D\\begin{vmatrix}A)P(A) + P(D \\mid A^{′})P(A^{′}) \\\\ 0.11|= (0.08)(0.70) + P(D \\mid A^{′})(0.30) \\\\ 0.110|= 0.056 + 0.3P(D \\mid A^{′}) \\\\ 0.3P(D \\mid A^{′})|= 0.054 \\\\ P(D\\end{vmatrix}A^{′})|= 0.18\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected component produced by machine $B$ is defective is $0.18$."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186082563 17592186082564 17592186082565 17592186082566 17592186082567 17592186082599 17592186082600 17592186082601 17592186082602 17592186082603 17592186082605 17592186082606 17592186082607 17592186082608 17592186082609] "immutable_fields" [[17592186082562 :answer-field/correct] [17592186082562 :answer-field/id] [17592186082562 :answer-field/key] [17592186082562 :answer-field/type] [17592186082598 :answer-field/correct] [17592186082598 :answer-field/id] [17592186082598 :answer-field/key] [17592186082598 :answer-field/type] [17592186082604 :answer-field/correct] [17592186082604 :answer-field/id] [17592186082604 :answer-field/key] [17592186082604 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
